-- title:
--   Orbit relation spreads from a field point to a localisation
-- statement:
--   The statement is made inside the full frame of the fine Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformization, whose hypotheses fall into the following groups.
--
--   **Numerical data.** Primes $r$ and $\bar r$ with $\bar r \neq r$, and a nonzero level $N$ divisible by neither $r$ nor $\bar r$.
--
--   **Coefficients.** A characteristic-zero domain $\mathcal O$ which is a discrete valuation ring, an irreducible element $\pi$, $\pi$-adic completeness of $\mathcal O$, residue ring of cardinality $r$, and the equality of ideals $(r) = (\pi)$; a characteristic-zero field $K_0$ which is the fraction field of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   **Unramified coefficients and Frobenius.** A characteristic-zero domain $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ over $\mathcal O$ with an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $\mathrm{Fr}$, such that $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ is complete for the ideal generated by $\pi$ (`hOnr_complete`), that ideal is maximal (`hOnr_max`), every element of $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ satisfies a monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_alg`), every monic polynomial over $O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ of positive degree has a root modulo $\pi$ (`hOnr_closed`), and $\mathrm{Fr}(x) \equiv x^{r} \pmod \pi$ for all $x$ (`hFr`). A homomorphism $v\det : \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0) \to \mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) characterised by `hvdet`: $v\det(g) = n$ iff $\det g = u\pi^{n}$ for some unit $u$ of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   **Quaternion algebra and moduli frame.** Rationals $a,b$ with `hB` asserting that $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]$ satisfies $0<a$ or $0<b$ and that, for a finite place $v$ of $\mathbb Q$, the completion is a division algebra exactly when $v$ lies above $r$ or above $\bar r$; a maximal order $\Lambda$ (`hΛ`). A scheme $\mathcal X$ with $f : \mathcal X \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and a point assignment `pt` sending a ring $S$, a morphism $s : \operatorname{Spec} S \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and a fake elliptic curve over $S$ with $\Lambda$-action and level-$N$ structure to a morphism over $s$; `h𝒳` asserts that this is a coarse moduli datum (invariance under isomorphism, compatibility with base change, bijectivity on geometric points over algebraically closed fields, and the universal property).
--
--   **Fine level structure.** An auxiliary level $n \geq 3$ with $r \nmid n$, $\bar r \nmid n$ and $\gcd(n,N)=1$; a scheme $M$ with $f_M : M \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and a point assignment `ptF` on fake elliptic curves with full level-$n$ structure, with `hM` asserting that `ptF` is isomorphism-invariant, compatible with base change, and bijective on $S$-points for every ring $S$. A group $G$, a homomorphism $\rho : G \to \operatorname{Aut} M$ and a labelling $\chi : G \to \Lambda$ with `hρ` asserting that $\rho$ acts over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$, that twisting a full level structure by $\chi(g)$ is realised by $\rho(g)$, and that $\chi$ is multiplicative modulo $n$ and induces a bijection onto the invertible classes modulo $n\Lambda$.
--
--   **Forgetful map, Hecke correspondences and Atkin–Lehner maps.** A morphism $p : M \to \mathcal X$ with $p$ followed by $f$ equal to $f_M$, invariant under $\rho$, and compatible with the two point assignments (`hp`, `hρp`, `hp_pt`). For each prime $\ell \notin \{r,\bar r\}$, a scheme $\mathcal Y_\ell$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ with point assignment `ptT` on fake elliptic curves with extra level-$\ell$ structure, coarse moduli (`h𝒴`), and two morphisms $d_0(\ell), d_1(\ell) : \mathcal Y_\ell \to \mathcal X$ over the base (`hd₀f`, `hd₁f`) which on points forget the extra structure, respectively send a point to the target of an $\ell$-level isogeny (`hd₀`, `hd₁`). Endomorphisms $a_r, a_{\bar r}$ of $\mathcal X$ over the base realising the Atkin–Lehner quotients at $r$ and at $\bar r$ on points (`har`, `harbar`).
--
--   **Definite quaternion side.** Rationals $a_1,b_1$ with `hdef` asserting $a_1<0$, $b_1<0$ and that $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]$ is a division algebra over the completion at a finite place exactly at the place above $\bar r$; a maximal order $\Lambda_1$, an Eichler order $R_1$ of level $N$ with $R_1 \leq \Lambda_1$; a finite-adelic unit $n_1$ in the $r$-th prime Hecke set of $R_1$, with $R_1 \cap n_1 R_1 n_1^{-1}$ (the `meetOrder`) an Eichler order of level $Nr$; an injective $\mathbb Q$-algebra embedding $\iota_0 : \mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1] \to M_2(K_0)$; a height-one prime $v$ of $\mathcal O_{\mathbb Q}$ containing $r$.
--
--   **Discrete group, Hecke elements and twisting data.** A subgroup $\tilde\Gamma$ of the units of $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]$ whose members are exactly the elements of `awayUnits R₁ v`, that is, those units lying at every finite place $w \neq v$ in the subgroup generated by the local unit box of $R_1$ at $w$. Families $s(\ell)$ of units and $sf(\ell)$ of finite-adelic units such that, for every $\ell$ (`hs`): $sf(\ell)$ has local component $s(\ell) \otimes 1$ at each place not containing $r$ and component $1$ at each place containing $r$; the product of the diagonal image of $\ell$ with $sf(\ell)^{-1}$ lies in `levelHeckeUSet Λ₁ (meetOrder R₁ n₁) ℓ` if $\ell \mid N$ and in `primeHeckeSet (meetOrder R₁ n₁) ℓ` otherwise; and $\mathrm{nrd}(s(\ell)) = \ell$. Subgroups $\tilde\Gamma_\ell = \tilde\Gamma \cap s(\ell)\tilde\Gamma s(\ell)^{-1}$. A unit $\bar w$ of reduced norm $\bar r$ normalising $\tilde\Gamma$ (`hwbar`). A homomorphism $\theta_t : \tilde\Gamma \to G$.
--
--   **The uniformization map.** A family $\Theta_f$ which, for every $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ in which $\pi$ is nilpotent, sends a triple $((\psi, P), g)$ consisting of an $\mathcal O$-algebra homomorphism $\psi : O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to B$, a Deligne datum $P$ over $B$ for $(\pi, K_0)$, and an element $g \in G$, to a $B$-point of $M$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$. It is required to be natural in $B$ (`hnat`); to satisfy $\rho(h)_*\Theta_f((\psi,P), gh) = \Theta_f((\psi,P), g)$ for all $g,h \in G$ (`hG`); to be invariant under the twisted $\tilde\Gamma$-action, in the sense that if $x'$ is obtained from $x$ by the relation `OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct` for $\iota_0(\gamma)$ with $\gamma \in \tilde\Gamma$ — namely $\psi' = \psi \circ \mathrm{Fr}^{-v\det \iota_0(\gamma)}$ and the Deligne datum of $x'$ is the pullback of that of $x$ along $\iota_0(\gamma)$ — then $\Theta_f(x', \theta_t(\gamma)g) = \Theta_f(x,g)$ (`hinv`); to be formally étale along surjections $p : B \to B_0$ with square-zero kernel, in the sense that every point of $M$ over $B$ lifting a $\Theta_f$-image over $B_0$ comes from a unique triple over $B$ lifting the given one (`het`); and, over an algebraically closed field $k$ in which $\pi$ is nilpotent and for a fixed $\psi : O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to k$, to be surjective onto the $k$-points of $M$ and to have as fibre relation exactly the $\tilde\Gamma$-orbit relation: $\Theta_f((\psi,P),g) = \Theta_f((\psi',P'),g')$ if and only if there is $\gamma \in \tilde\Gamma$ with $g' = \theta_t(\gamma)g$, with $P'$ the pullback of $P$ along $\iota_0(\gamma)^{-1}$, and with $\psi'(y) = \psi(\mathrm{Fr}^{-v\det \iota_0(\gamma)}y)$ for every $y \in O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ fixed by $\mathrm{Fr}^{v\det \iota_0(z)}$ for all central $z \in \tilde\Gamma$ (those with $z = c\cdot 1$ for some $c \in \mathbb Q$) satisfying $\theta_t(z) = 1$ (`hgeom`).
--
--   **Conclusion.** For every $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ in which $\pi$ is nilpotent, all pairs $x_1 = (\psi_1, P_1)$ and $x_2 = (\psi_2, P_2)$ over $B$ and all $g_1, g_2 \in G$ with $\Theta_f(x_1,g_1) = \Theta_f(x_2,g_2)$, every field $L$ over $\mathcal O$, every $\mathcal O$-algebra homomorphism $\varphi : B \to L$ and every $\gamma \in \tilde\Gamma$, the following holds. Assume that after base change along $\varphi$ the three relations attached to $\gamma$ hold, namely: the Deligne datum $\varphi_*P_2$ is the pullback of $\varphi_*P_1$ along $\iota_0(\gamma)^{-1}$; for every $y \in O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ fixed by $\mathrm{Fr}^{v\det \iota_0(z)}$ for all central $z \in \tilde\Gamma$ with $\theta_t(z)=1$, one has $(\varphi\circ\psi_2)(y) = (\varphi \circ \psi_1)(\mathrm{Fr}^{-v\det \iota_0(\gamma)}y)$; and $g_2 = \theta_t(\gamma)g_1$. Then there exists $f \in B$ with $\varphi(f) \neq 0$ such that for every $\mathcal O$-algebra $C$ and every $\mathcal O$-algebra homomorphism $\chi : B \to C$ with $\chi(f)$ a unit, the same three relations hold after base change along $\chi$ and for the same $\gamma$: the Deligne datum $\chi_*P_2$ is the pullback of $\chi_*P_1$ along $\iota_0(\gamma)^{-1}$; for every $y \in O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ fixed by $\mathrm{Fr}^{v\det \iota_0(z)}$ for all central $z \in \tilde\Gamma$ with $\theta_t(z)=1$, one has $(\chi\circ\psi_2)(y) = (\chi\circ\psi_1)(\mathrm{Fr}^{-v\det \iota_0(\gamma)}y)$; and $g_2 = \theta_t(\gamma)g_1$.
--
--   Thus the orbit relation witnessed by $\gamma$ at the point $\ker\varphi$ persists over the principal localisation $B[1/f]$, and in particular over every $\mathcal O$-algebra in which $f$ becomes invertible.
--
--   This is the spreading-out step in the identification of the fibres of the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformization of the fine moduli scheme of fake elliptic curves with full level-$n$ structure: a $\tilde\Gamma$-orbit relation known at a single field-valued point of $\operatorname{Spec} B$ is propagated to a Zariski neighbourhood of that point. It is used by [`CerednikDrinfeld.QM.IsFineModuli.exists_flat_family_isPullback_of_cerednikDrinfeld_uniformization_fine_eq`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.QM.IsFineModuli.exists_flat_family_isPullback_of_cerednikDrinfeld_uniformization_fine_eq), where such neighbourhoods are combined over a quasi-compact base.
